#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""timbersh — an interactive shell for timberfs.

⚠ EXPERIMENTAL, and the grammar is not promised. It exists to be USED
against a real fleet, because a protocol nobody writes a client against
is a protocol whose awkward parts stay theoretical — and every awkward
part it has found so far became a fix in timberfs. Expect statements to
change under you; nothing here is a compatibility surface yet.


    create logview [type=console] console;
    select stores  from console;
    select records from console where entry has 'ERROR' limit 100;
    select records from [] where chunk may have 'req-8f3a';
    tail   records from console;
    view   [type=console] at '12:00';

A LOGVIEW is a NAMED PREDICATE, re-resolved every time it is used — so a
store that appears after you defined it is in it. That is the whole
reason the selection is a query and not a list.

`[...]` is a predicate literal, so a view name and a label can never be
mistaken for one another, and a completer always knows which it is
completing.

The SELECTOR is deliberately not implemented: every resolution is a real
`kind: "stores"` query, because a second copy of it would drift and a
drifted selector answers a different question without saying so.

`view` is a PAGER over one store rather than a query, and it runs in
process: the loop is search, jump, search again, and a separate program
would have to leave the screen on every hop. `timberview` is the same
module with its own entry point.

Times ARE resolved at this edge, and must be — `timberview.when`, shared
by both front ends. A query document is meant to be SELF-CONTAINED, so
building one cannot require running a timberfs on THIS machine to
interpret a string. `window.from` is milliseconds because a document is
a value: `11:10` means today, and in the reader's timezone, so a
document carrying the text would mean something different tomorrow and
something else again at the far end.

`--help` lists the flags; each has an environment variable beside it.

  TIMBERFS_RESOLVER
                 a command that prints the fleet as a target document,
                 or a url to GET it from:

                   TIMBERFS_RESOLVER='fleet-sweep --json'
                   TIMBERFS_RESOLVER=https://fleet.internal/targets
                   TIMBERFS_RESOLVER=unix+http:///run/agent.sock//fleet

                 A url is told from a command by its SCHEME.
  TIMBERFS_CMD   argv prefix, default `timberfs query --query -`
  TIMBERFS_HOSTS comma-separated hosts to fan out to. Every occurrence of
                 _TIMBERHOST_ in TIMBERFS_CMD is replaced with the host:

                   TIMBERFS_HOSTS=web01,web02,db01
                   TIMBERFS_CMD="ssh _TIMBERHOST_ timberfs query --query -"

                 Any command that reaches a timberfs works, so a wrapper
                 that takes the host as an argument does too. A target
                 document (TIMBERFS_RESOLVER, TIMBERFS_TARGETS) can name
                 a `url` instead: http://, https:// or
                 unix+http:///path/to.sock//request/path

                 Stores from every host are presented as one set, and each
                 remembers where it lives. Unset, there is one unnamed host
                 and nothing is substituted.
  TIMBERFS_REFRESH
                 how `refresh` re-derives the fleet, where that is not
                 how it was produced. A command or a url, like the
                 resolver
  TIMBERSH_RC    statements run at startup, default ~/.timbershrc
  TIMBERSH_HISTFILE
                 line history, default ~/.timbersh-history, mode 0600
  TIMBERSH_HISTSIZE
                 how many lines to keep, default 2000
  TIMBERSH_PAGE  entries per CALL on an unbounded read, default 10000 --
                 not the statement's `limit`, which is how much of the
                 answer you want
"""
import argparse, json, os, queue, re, readline, sys, threading, time

# The viewer, and the pieces both front ends share. A checkout finds
# them beside this script; the package installs them where both do.
_HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
sys.path[:0] = [p for p in (_HERE, "/usr/share/timberfs-sh")
                if os.path.exists(os.path.join(p, "timberview.py"))]
import timberfs_client
import timberview
from timberfs_client import HOST_TOKEN, when, when_ms

